---
title: Save file
sidebar:
  order: 2
---

For most things your mod needs to remember, a [saved flag or byte](/papermario-dx/save-data/game-flags) is enough. When it isn't, such as for a number bigger than 127 or a list of values, you can add a field to the save file itself.

The save file is the `SaveData` struct in `dx/versioning.h`. The game writes it to the save slot exactly as it's laid out in memory, and reads it back the same way. If you move or resize a field, a save from before the change reads its data into the wrong fields.

## Adding a field

`SaveData` ends with unused padding, `PAD(0x7C)`. Take your field's space from it, so every other field stays where it was:

```c
    /* 0x12EC */ SaveFileSummary summary;
    /* 0x1304 */ s32 fishCaught; // [!code ++]
    /* 0x1308 */ PAD(0x78); // [!code ++]
} SaveData; // size = 0x1380
```

The padding is zero in existing saves, so in a save from before the change, the new field starts at `0`.

Read and write the field through `gCurrentSaveFile`, such as `gCurrentSaveFile.fishCaught++`. It's saved whenever the player saves.

Starting a new game doesn't clear the field by itself, so a value from a file loaded earlier would carry over. To reset it, add a line such as `saveFile->fishCaught = 0;` to `clear_saved_variables` in `engine2/vars_access.c`.

### Player data

`PlayerData` in `common_structs.h`, which holds the player's stats and inventory, also ends with unused space, `u8 reserved[0xE8]`. You can take a field's space from it the same way:

```c
    /* 0x33E */ PAD(2);
    /* 0x340 */ s32 racesWon; // [!code ++]
    /* 0x344 */ u8 reserved[0xE4]; // [!code ++]
} PlayerData; // size = 0x428
```

The game copies `gPlayerData` into the save when the player saves, and copies the save's `PlayerData` back into `gPlayerData` when a save loads. Read and write the field through `gPlayerData`, such as `gPlayerData.racesWon++`. To reset it when a new game starts, set it in `clear_player_data` in `engine1/inventory.c`.

## Changing the layout

Some changes can't keep the layout, such as making an existing array bigger, like the `invItems` array in `PlayerData`, or reordering items. For these, increase `DX_MOD_VER_MAJOR` in `dx/config.h`. Every save records the version of your mod that wrote it, and `fio_deserialize_state` in `dx/versioning.c` checks it when a save loads:

```c
} else if (saveData->majorVersion != DX_MOD_VER_MAJOR) {
    // handle breaking changes between major versions here
    ver_deserialize_standard(saveData);
}
```

By default, an older save loads as if nothing had changed. To keep older saves working, replace that call with code that copies each field from the old layout to the new one. `ver_deserialize_vanilla_save` in the same file does this for saves from the original game, so you can use it as a model.

If your mod hasn't been released yet, no one has older saves, so you can change the layout freely.
